  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\nA closed upper level low is approaching Montana from the west this\nmorning. Thus expect increasing clouds this afternoon, along with\nincreasing chances for showers/thunderstorms by this evening. The\nheaviest precipitation will generally fall over North Central MT\ntonight. Colder temperatures will also move into the CWA, with the\nGlacier Park area seeing the coldest temperatures. Rain will\nchange to snow for elevations above 6500 feet overnight, with some\nsnow accumulations at ridge tops. This system exits the CWA on\nMonday, with showers decreasing during the afternoon.\n\nGenerally dry and stable conditions are expected from Tue through\nThursday, along with a slow warming trend during this period.\n\nOn Friday, a quick moving closed upper level low will move from\nwest to east across Southern MT. This will result in a good chance\nfor precipitation over Southwest MT by Friday. At this time, most\nof the precipitation will fall as rain, and since it will be \nquick moving, heavy precipitation is not expected at this time.\n\nLooking ahead to next weekend, generally dry and warm conditions\nare expected for next Sat/Sun, as a quick building upper level\nridge of high pressure is forecast to build over the northern\nRockies.\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\nThe main concern for later today and tonight will be thunderstorm\npotential over the Plains and snow over the Rockies. \n\nThe airmass will become unstable by this afternoon, with the HREF\nforecasting a strong storm developing between Great Falls and\nHavre between 5 PM and 8 PM this evening. Hail and strong wind\ngusts will be the main impact from this storm. Additional\nthunderstorms are possible a bit further east this evening as the\nentire disturbance starts to shift to the east.\n\nOvernight, the concern shifts to the Rocky Mountain Front in terms\nof how low do snow levels get by Monday morning. The current\nfreezing level aloft is near 11,000 feet, so it will take some\ntime for the air to cool this evening. That being said, by Monday\nmorning, snow levels could fall down to about 6500 feet over the\nRocky Mountain Front, including the Glacier Park area. Snow\naccumulations of a few inches are possible above 7000 feet. No\nwinter weather advisory for snow will be issued at this time.\n\nThere continues to be near a 70 percent chance for strong winds\ntoday from Ennis to Norris Hill. It is looking like sustained\nwinds for an hour or so might be the bigger impact. Thus a high\nwind warning has been issued for this area.\n\nFor Monday, there continues to be about a 60 percent chance for\nstrong winds over Judith Basin and Fergus counties. Thus the high\nwind watch over Central MT was expanded to include Judith Basin\nCounty for Monday. Brusda",
